## Summary

Allow an operator to explicitly approve an externally managed copy of a shipped Codex skill without granting codex-router ownership of that directory.

This fixes the case where the same reviewed cross-client skills are managed outside codex-router (for example, shared across Codex/Copilot/Claude) and therefore correctly fail the existing ownership check, but still satisfy the router's required skill contract.

## Security model

This does **not** weaken the existing fail-closed ownership boundary:

- router-owned skills still require the protected private ownership record plus the matching random on-disk marker;
- external coexistence is a separate explicit approval path and never creates an ownership marker;
- approval binds both the exact external directory tree digest and the exact corresponding router-shipped skill directory digest;
- symlinks and unsupported filesystem entries are rejected;
- any external-content change or router skill-source change invalidates approval and returns the skill to collision/missing status until it is reviewed again;
- unknown or malformed content is never auto-adopted;
- uninstall removes only verified router-owned skills and preserves approved external directories;
- malformed protected state continues to fail closed.

The CLI adds `approve-external` and `revoke-external`. Doctor now distinguishes verified managed skills, approved external skills, stale external approvals, and unverified collisions, and prints the explicit review/approval command for collisions.

## Verification

