### What happened?

In a native `gpt-6-luna` session, historical tool results keep being evaluated but no archives are created, despite Token maxxing and “Compact native results too” being enabled. A controlled reproduction isolates a compatibility gap: recovery checks only top-level `payload.tools` and skips supported tool declarations carried in Responses Lite `additional_tools` input items.

### Environment

- Router revision: `2e3fd008a130d5adf3e2a4b690780abcdb3e4602`.
- Client: Codex Desktop on macOS; native OpenAI/ChatGPT route, `gpt-6-luna`.
- Model cache: `use_responses_lite: true`.
- Aging settings: `enabled: true`, `nativeEnabled: true`, `retentionTtlDays: 0`.

### Actual and expected behavior

At inspection, the usage ledger recorded 120 evaluated requests for this model, a largest evaluated result of 48,026 bytes, zero results aged, and zero bytes saved. The configured archive directory contained no `.result` files.

Earlier metadata-only instrumentation of a native request recorded `hasTools: false`, `toolChoice: "auto"`, and `inputCount: 131`. The complete input array from that exact request was not captured. The saved-session declaration and synthetic reproduction below establish the format mismatch and failing code path; they are not a replay of the complete failing Desktop request.

Expected: an eligible result should reach archival when the request declares a supported local reader tool, including through Responses Lite. Its full text should remain until storage succeeds and the client returns a valid signed readback proof. Recognizing the tool inventory must not itself permit omission.

### Source trace

Codex's [Responses Lite request builder](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/09db085f9456c608a7960fdd4c5c55a34c6a84ea/codex-rs/core/src/client.rs#L1057-L1072) puts declarations in developer-role `additional_tools` input items and sets top-level tools to `None`; [serialization omits that field](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/09db085f9456c608a7960fdd4c5c55a34c6a84ea/codex-rs/codex-api/src/common.rs#L280-L291). The saved session contains such a declaration with `functions.exec` and its `tools.exec_command` description. The existing recognizer accepts that inventory when given it directly.

1. [The native handler](https://github.com/duolahypercho/codex-router/blob/2e3fd008a130d5adf3e2a4b690780abcdb3e4602/src/router.mjs#L4956-L4963) calls `toolResultReadbackTools(payload)`.
2. [The helper](https://github.com/duolahypercho/codex-router/blob/2e3fd008a130d5adf3e2a4b690780abcdb3e4602/src/tool-result-recovery.mjs#L117-L120) reads only `payload.tools`, so it returns undefined.
3. [The recovery wrapper](https://github.com/duolahypercho/codex-router/blob/2e3fd008a130d5adf3e2a4b690780abcdb3e4602/src/tool-result-recovery.mjs#L343-L352) takes its no-local-tool fallback before candidate planning or retention. The pure aging policy still reports evaluation counts, but has no retention callback with which to compact output.

The streaming parser preserves the input declarations. The reproduction below asserts that, and controls for eligibility: the 48,026-byte result is outside the newest-four frontier and has a later model action.

### Reproduction

Run from the repository root with its dependencies installed. The fixture is synthetic; no tool is executed. The retention callback only counts attempts and returns undefined, so the script makes no model requests or archive writes.

